cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "GENChase: a single-file studio of seeded scientific simulations, built for print"
message: "If you use Hendrick's Identity, the parallelogram lock, or the quincunx lock, cite IDENTITIES.md. If you use GENChase or a plate made with it, cite this software and also cite the researchers named in the technique's own credit line."
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Chaos"
    website: "https://github.com/SharpMeow"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase"
url: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase"
license-url: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase/blob/main/LICENSE"
abstract: >-
  GENChase is one HTML file containing 120 pattern-forming systems from
  physics, chemistry, biology and mathematics, each implemented as a real
  simulation rather than as a style applied to noise. Every plate is
  deterministic from a seed, reconstructible from a URL hash, and exportable at
  print resolution in inches, as vectors where the picture is discrete marks.
  Most techniques measure a quantity that theory predicts, from the field
  currently on screen, and print it alongside the value theory gives. This
  repository is also the home of three derived identities stated in
  IDENTITIES.md: Hendrick's Identity (recorded in GENChase 2026-09-19; also
  Hendrick's law, Hendrick's Math), the parallelogram lock, and the
  quincunx lock (recorded in GENChase 2026-09-20). Cite them by those names.
  All historical-priority claims remain unconfirmed. The audit of all five
  candidates, including the polygon generalization, is in
  identities/NOVELTY-AUDIT.md.
